    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "Consider the following C-function in which `a[n]` and `b[m]` are two sorted integer arrays and `c[n+m]` be another integer array. ```c void xyz(int a[], int b[], int c[]) {     int i, j, k;     i = j = k = 0;     while ((i < n) && (j < m))         if (a[i] < b[j])             c[k++] = a[i++];         else             c[k++] = b[j++]; } ``` Which of the following condition(s) hold(s) after the termination of the while loop? I. \\(j < m\\), \\(k = n + j - 1\\), and \\(a[n-1] < b[j]\\) if \\(i = n\\) II. \\(i < n\\), \\(k = m + i - 1\\), and \\(b[m-1] \\leq a[i]\\) if \\(j = m\\)",
    image: null,
    options: ["only (I)", "only (II)", "either (I) or (II) but not both", "neither (I) nor (II)"],
    answer: "either (I) or (II) but not both",
    explanation: "The loop terminates as soon as either `i == n` or `j == m`.  **Case 1**: The loop terminates because `i == n`. This means all elements of array `a` were copied to `c`. The last element copied was `a[n-1]`. Since it was copied before some elements of `b` were exhausted, it must be that \\(a[n-1] < b[j]\\). Also, \\(k = n + j - 1\\). This strictly satisfies condition I.  **Case 2**: The loop terminates because `j == m`. This means all elements of `b` were copied. The last element copied was `b[m-1]`. By the `else` logic, this happens if \\(b[m-1] \\leq a[i]\\). Also, \\(k = m + i - 1\\). This strictly satisfies condition II.  Since only one array triggers the termination of the `while` condition first, exactly one of these cases will reflect the final state. Thus, either (I) or (II) holds, but not both.  Quick Tip When merging sorted arrays, the loop stops as soon as one array is exhausted; the remaining elements belong to the other array."
