    explanation: "Solution The hint tells us:  len1 = length of the current consecutive run of the same value.  len2 = length of the longest subarray ending at X[i] containing at most two distinct values. Case 1: X[i] == first The current element is the same as the previous element. So both lengths simply increase by 1:  len1=len1+1  len2=len2+1 This case is already given. Case 2: X[i] == second The current element is the second distinct value. For example:  1,1,1,2 Now suppose another 2 arrives:  1,1,1,2,2 The valid subarray still contains only two distinct values, so:  len2=len2+1 But the current consecutive run is now the run of 2s. Since this new run starts at the current element, we reset:  len1=1 Therefore,  boxedP=1 Case 3: X[i] is a new third value Suppose the current valid subarray is:  1,1,1,2,2 Here:  len2=5 and the latest consecutive run is:  len1=2 Now suppose 3 arrives:  1,1,1,2,2,3 This has three distinct values, so we cannot keep the whole previous subarray. We must discard the older value 1 and keep:  2,2,3 The length is therefore:  len1+1 Hence,  boxedQ=len1+1 Why not len2+1? If we used len2+1, the example would give:  5+1=6 But [1,1,1,2,2,3] contains three distinct values, so it is not valid. The only part of the previous subarray that can be retained is the latest consecutive run, whose length is len1. Therefore,  Q=len1+1 Final Answer boxedP=1,  Q=len1+1 Therefore,  boxedtextbfOption B"
