    question: "Consider the following C function definition. ```c int fX(char *a) {     char *b = a;     while(*b)     b++;     return b - a; } ``` Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?",
    image: null,
    options: ["The function call fX(\"abcd\") will always return a value", "Assuming a character array c is declared as char c[] = \"abcd\" in main(), the function call fX(c)will always return a value", "The code of the function will not compile", "Assuming a character pointer c is declared as char *c = \"abcd\" in main(), the function call fX(c)will always return a value"],
    answer: "The function call fX(\"abcd\") will always return a value, Assuming a character array c is declared as char c[] = \"abcd\" in main(), the function call fX(c)will always return a value, Assuming a character pointer c is declared as char *c = \"abcd\" in main(), the function call fX(c)will always return a value",
    explanation: "Derivation This question evaluates the mechanics of pointers and null-terminated strings in the C language.  Examine the function: `char *b = a; while(*b) b++; return b - a;` This logic implements a custom string length calculation. The loop advances the pointer `b` as long as it points to a non-zero character. It terminates exactly when `*b` hits the null terminator `\\0`. The operation `b - a` calculates the pointer difference, yielding the exact length of the string.  Analyzing the statements: **A)** `fX(\"abcd\")` passes a string literal. String literals in C are automatically null-terminated. The loop halts at `\\0` and returns the length. (True) **B)** `char c[] = \"abcd\"` initializes a character array with the string literal, automatically appending the implicit null terminator. The function executes flawlessly. (True) **C)** \"The code will not compile\" is incorrect. The syntax is perfectly valid C pointer arithmetic. (False) **D)** `char *c = \"abcd\"` assigns a pointer to a read-only memory segment containing the null-terminated string. The function correctly reads the memory segment and returns the length. (True)  Therefore, statements A, B, and D are valid.  Common Trap Aspirants are occasionally alarmed by the final statement `return b - a;`. They assume subtracting memory addresses will yield a massive, unpredictable memory offset error. However, pointer arithmetic in C automatically divides the address difference by the size of the base data type, returning the exact number of elements.  AIR-1 Insight When tracing pointer variables in character parameters, remember the fundamental C rule: all standard string declarations (whether initialized as arrays or pointer literals) are strictly contiguous memory blocks capped with a `\\0` suffix.  Quick Tip No quick tip available"
