    id: "PYQ531", year: 2026, subject: "Compiler Design", topic: "Syntax Directed Translation",
    type: "MSQ", marks: 2,
    question: "Consider the following two syntax-directed definitions SDD1 and SDD2 for type declarations.  Syntax-Directed Definition 1 (SDD1)  | Grammar (G1) | Semantic Rules | | :--- | :--- | | D -> T V | D.type = T.type <br> V.type = T.type | | T -> int | T.type = int | | T -> float | T.type = float | | V -> V1 id | V1.type = V.type <br> put(id.entry, V.type) | | V -> id | put(id.entry, V.type) |  Syntax-Directed Definition 2 (SDD2)  | Grammar (G2) | Semantic Rules | | :--- | :--- | | D -> D1 id | D.type = D1.type <br> put(id.entry, D1.type) | | D -> T id | D.type = T.type <br> put(id.entry, T.type) | | T -> int | T.type = int | | T -> float | T.type = float |  D is the start symbol, and int, float, and id are the three terminals. The non-terminal V_1 is the same as V and the non-terminal D_1 is the same as D. Here, the subscript is used to differentiate the grammar symbols on the two sides of a production. The function put updates the symbol table with the type information for an identifier.  Let P and Q be the languages specified by grammars G1 and G2, respectively. Which of the following statements is/are true?",
    image: null,
    options: ["The languages P and Q are the same", "SDD2 is S-attributed and contains only synthesized attributes", "SDD1 is L-attributed and contains only inherited attributes", "The specifications of SDD1 and SDD2 are such that the same entries get added to the symbol table"],
    answer: "The languages P and Q are the same, SDD2 is S-attributed and contains only synthesized attributes, The specifications of SDD1 and SDD2 are such that the same entries get added to the symbol table",
    explanation: "Evaluation of Statements  * **(a) True:** Both grammars generate identical languages, which consist of declarations of the structural form: (textint mid textfloat) text id^+ This denotes that a singular type specifier is followed sequentially by one or more identifiers.     * In textSDD_1, the underlying context-free grammar is defined by D → TV, where T → textint mid textfloat  and V → V_1 text id mid textid. Thus, selecting a base type yields a sequence containing one or more identifiers.     * In textSDD_2, the production rules yield the exact same language format. Here, the list of identifiers is expanded recursively using the rule D → D_1 text id with the base operational case D → T text id. Consequently, this grammar also enforces a single type declaration followed by a chain of identifiers. Hence, the language equivalence holds: P = Q.  * **(b) True:** textSDD_2 is strictly S-attributed because it computes semantic details exclusively via synthesized attributes. In textSDD_2, the evaluation rules are explicitly declared as:     * T.texttype = textint or T.texttype = textfloat     * D.texttype = T.texttype (for D → T text id)     * D.texttype = D_1.texttype (for D → D_1 text id) Here, type attributes propagate upstream within the parse tree, moving exclusively from child nodes to parent nodes. Because no semantic data is passed downward from a parent to a child or horizontally across sibling dependencies, only synthesized attributes are present. Therefore, textSDD_2 is S-attributed.  * **(c) False:** textSDD_1 is not an S-attributed definition. It is classified as an L-attributed definition because its evaluation depends on a combination of both synthesized and inherited attributes.     * In textSDD_1, the attribute T.texttype is synthesized upward from the terminal token.      * This type attribute is subsequently passed into the structural branch of the non-terminal V at the production level D → TV through the assignment rule V.texttype = T.texttype.      * Following this, within the production rule V → V_1 text id, the evaluation rule V_1.texttype = V.texttype routes the type information downward to the child node.  Because structural attributes are transmitted down the dependency graph, textSDD_1 relies on inherited attributes, violating the constraints of an S-attributed system. Hence, statement (c) is false.  * **(d) True:** Both textSDD_1 and textSDD_2 perform identical side-effect actions on the underlying symbol table.     * In textSDD_1, once the active type parameter is resolved, every distinct identifier within the V-chain is cataloged via the operational action textput(textid.entry, V.texttype).     * In textSDD_2, each individual identifier is inserted symmetrically using either textput(textid.entry, T.texttype) or textput(textid.entry, D_1.texttype).  Since the attribute value of D_1.texttype is derived from the core type evaluated at node T, all identifiers are mapped to their correct type specifications. Thus, for any syntactically valid declaration string, both definitions insert identical identifier-type attribute pairs into the symbol table.  **Conclusion:** Therefore, statements **(a), (b), and (d)** are mathematically true, whereas statement **(c)** is false. Quick Tip No quick tip available"
