    answer: "128.8.129.43 and 128.8.161.55",
    explanation: "Background Required - **Subnets and Subnet Masks**  **Answer:** D  Explanation  Suppose we have a host A with IP Address IP_A, and the subnet mask of the subnet of which A is a part is M_s.   When A wants to send a packet to a host B with an IP address IP_B, then A will first perform a bitwise **AND** operation of the subnet mask M_s with IP_A to find the subnet ID (ID_N) of the subnet, in order to identify whether IP_B belongs to the same network:    ID_N = IP_A text AND  M_s     If B also belongs to the same network, then IP_B bitwise **AND** M_s must be equal to the calculated ID_N:    IP_B text AND  M_s = ID_N     Therefore, if IP_A and IP_B belong to the same network, we must have:    IP_A text AND  M_s = IP_B text AND  M_s     Out of the four options, only option D satisfies this condition:  - 128.8.129.43 text AND  255.255.31.0 = 128.8.1.0 - 128.8.161.55 text AND  255.255.31.0 = 128.8.1.0 Quick Tip For subnet mask problems, focus on the bits where the mask is not all 255s or 0s. This is where the subnetting magic happens!"
