    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "Consider a token ring topology with N stations (numbered 1 to N) running token ring protocol where the stations are equally spaced. When a station gets the token it is allowed to send one frame of fixed size. Ring latency is t_p , while the transmission time of a frame is t_t . All other latencies can be neglected. The maximum utilization of the token ring when t_t = 5 ms, t_p = 3 ms, N = 15 is:",
    image: null,
    options: ["0.545", "0.655", "0.9375", "0.961"],
    answer: "0.961",
    explanation: "In a Token Ring network, maximum utilization occurs when all N stations have data to send. Since t_t (5 text ms) > t_p (3 text ms), the token is released immediately after transmission finishes (effectively early token release behavior since the frame covers the ring). The time utilized for transmission in one cycle is N × t_t. The total time for one cycle is the transmission time plus the total ring propagation delay to pass the token across all stations: N × t_t + t_p. Max Utilization U = fracN · t_tN · t_t + t_p = frac15 × 515 × 5 + 3 = frac7578 approx 0.9615. Quick Tip Use elimination: only options near 0.9 are plausible for N=15 with t_t>t_p."
