    answer: "II and III are the preorder and inorder sequences, respectively",
    explanation: "Step 1: Identify the root  In preorder traversal, the root is visited first.  In postorder traversal, the root is visited last.  Hence, the first element of the preorder sequence must equal the last element of the postorder sequence.  Write the sequences with their first and last elements:  I. M B C A F H P Y K  First = M, Last = K  II. K A M C B Y P F H  First = K, Last = H  III. M A B C K Y F P H  First = M, Last = H  The only possible matching pair is:  II starts with K I ends with K  Therefore:  II = Preorder  I = Postorder  Step 2: Identify the remaining sequence  Since every sequence is used exactly once, the remaining sequence must be the inorder traversal.  Therefore:  III = Inorder  So the assignments are:  Preorder = II = KAMCBYPFH  Inorder = III = MABCKYFPH  Postorder = I = MBCAFHPYK  Step 3: Match with the options  Option D says:  \"II and III are the preorder and inorder sequences, respectively.\"  This exactly matches our result.  Therefore:  ## Official Answer Check  This is GATE CSE/IT 2008, Question 46. Published GATE solution material gives the answer as **D**, with the same identification:  Preorder = II, Inorder = III, Postorder = I.  ## Final Answer  boxedtextD  ## 💡 Quick Tip  For this type of question, you usually do not need to construct the tree.  Just compare:  textFirst element of preorder = textLast element of postorder  Here:  textII starts with K = textI ends with K  So immediately:  II = Preorder   I = Postorder   III = Inorder  Hence D."
