    id: "PYQ1163", year: 2014, subject: "Data Structures", topic: "Avl Tree",
    type: "NAT", marks: 2,
    question: "Suppose we have a balanced binary search tree T holding n numbers. We are given two numbers L and H and wish to sum up all the numbers in T that lie between L and H. Suppose there are m such numbers in T. If the tightest upper bound on the time to compute the sum is ``` O(n^{a}log^{b} n+m^{c}log^{d} n) ``` the value of a + 10b + 100c + 1000d is _______.",
    image: null,
    options: [],
    answer: "110",
    explanation: "Problem Analysis  - We are given a balanced binary search tree T with n nodes. - The goal is to sum all nodes within the range [L, H]. - Let m represent the number of nodes within the specified range. - We need to determine the tightest upper bound in the form O(n^a log^b n + m^c log^d n). Range Query in a Balanced BST  - To identify nodes in the range [L, H], we can employ an in-order traversal with range constraints:   - Locate the first node with a value ≥ L. This operation takes O(log n) time, leveraging the balanced nature of the BST.   - Locate the last node with a value ≤ H. This also takes O(log n) time.   - Traverse all nodes within the identified range [L, H]. This step requires O(m) time, as we visit each of the m nodes once.  - Therefore, the overall time complexity is O(log n + m). Matching the Given Form  - The problem requires expressing the complexity in the form O(n^a log^b n + m^c log^d n). - While our derived complexity O(log n + m) is tighter, we need to fit it into the provided format. -  The tightest upper bound, adhering to the given form, can be obtained by considering:   -  Potentially traversing the entire tree. Although the initial node location takes O(log n) time, in the *worst* case, the relevant range can force visiting every node, so a component scaling with n is needed.   - Summing the m nodes within the range, which takes O(m) time.  - Thus, the tightest upper bound conforming to the required form is O(n + m). Note that, if we visit a node which turns out not to be in the range [L, H], we can stop the search from that node onwards. This can be shown to be O(n).  - This corresponds to:   - a = 1, b = 0, c = 1, d = 0.  - Consequently: a + 10b + 100c + 1000d = 1 + 10 · 0 + 100 · 1 + 1000 · 0 = 101. Therefore, the answer is 101. Quick Tip Eliminate options with a > 0 or c > 1"
