    question: "Consider rooted n node binary tree represented using pointers. The best upper bound on the time required to determine the number of sub trees having exactly 4 nodes is ```text O(n^{a}log^{b}n) ``` . Then the value of a+10b is_______",
    image: null,
    options: [],
    answer: "1",
    explanation: "Solution A subtree rooted at a node consists of that node and all its descendants. To determine the number of subtrees having exactly 4 nodes, we can perform a **single postorder traversal** of the binary tree. During the traversal:  - Compute the size of the left subtree. - Compute the size of the right subtree. - The size of the subtree rooted at the current node is  textsize=1+textleft size+textright size If the computed size equals 4, increment the answer. Since each node is visited exactly once and only constant work is performed at each node, the total time complexity is  O(n). Thus,  a=1,  b=0 Therefore,  a+10b=1+10(0)=1 Final Answer boxed1  ## 💡 Quick Tip Whenever a tree problem asks for information about every subtree, think of a **postorder traversal**. It allows information from the children to be computed before processing the parent, often yielding an O(n) solution.  ## ⚠️ Common Mistake Do not compute the size of every subtree independently using a separate DFS from each node. That approach takes  O(n^2) in the worst case. A single postorder traversal computes all subtree sizes simultaneously in linear time."
