    explanation: "Max-Heap Conversion - Array: (89, 19, 50, 17, 12, 15, 2, 5, 7, 11, 6, 9, 100) - Size: 13 - Last non-leaf node: 13/2 = 6.5, so index 6 (value 15) - Heapify from index 6 down to 1.  - Index 6 (15): Children are 11(index 11) and 9(index 12). Max(15,11,9) = 15. No swap. - Index 5 (12): Children are 6(index 10) and 9(index 11). Max(12,6,9) = 12. No swap. - Index 4 (17): Children are 5(index 8) and 7(index 9). Max(17,5,7) = 17. No swap. - Index 3 (50): Children are 2(index 6) and 15(index 7). Max(50,2,15) = 50. No swap. - Index 2 (19): Children are 11(index 4) and 6(index 5). Max(19,17,12) = 19. No swap. - Index 1 (89): Children are 19(index 2) and 50(index 3). Max(89,19,50) = 89. No swap.  - Now, the last element 100 is at the end. We need to swap it with the root. - Swap 89 and 100. Array: (100, 19, 50, 17, 12, 15, 2, 5, 7, 11, 6, 9, 89). Count = 1 - Heapify 89 from index 1. - Children of 89 are 19 and 50. Swap 89 and 50. Array: (100, 19, 89, 17, 12, 15, 2, 5, 7, 11, 6, 9, 50). Count = 2 - Children of 89 are 5 and 7. Swap 89 and 7. Array: (100, 19, 7, 17, 12, 15, 2, 5, 89, 11, 6, 9, 50). Count = 3 - Children of 89 are none.   Therefore, 3 swaps are needed. Quick Tip Use the elimination trick: if a swap count is odd, the answer must be the odd option (B or D)."
