    explanation: "In a max heap, a new element is first inserted at the **next available position** to maintain the complete binary tree. The initial heap is:  ```              40           /     \\\\         30      20        /  \\    /   \\\\      10   15  16   17     /  \\\\    8   4  ``` The next available position is the **left child of 15**. So insert 35: ```              40           /     \\\\         30      20        /  \\    /   \\\\      10   15  16   17     / \\   /    8   4 35  ``` The array is now     \\40,30,20,10,15,16,17,8,4,35\\     Now we perform **up-heapification**. Step 1: Compare 35 with its parent The parent of the element at index 9 is at index     leftlfloorfrac9-12rightrfloor=4     The value at index 4 is 15. Since   35>15,     swap 35 and 15. The array becomes     \\40,30,20,10,35,16,17,8,4,15\\  Step 2: Continue upward Now 35 is at index 4. Its parent is at     leftlfloorfrac4-12rightrfloor=1     The value at index 1 is 30. Since   35>30,     swap them. The array becomes     \\40,35,20,10,30,16,17,8,4,15\\  Step 3: Check the new parent Now 35 is at index 1. Its parent is 40. Since   35<40,     the max-heap property is satisfied. Therefore, no more swaps are required. Final Answer $  boxedtextbfB     boxed\\40,35,20,10,30,16,17,8,4,15\\   ## 💡 Quick Tip For insertion into a heap:     boxedtextInsert at the end → textCompare with parent → textSwap upward     The element always starts at the **end of the array**. Do not choose its position based on which child looks visually appropriate.  ## ⚠️ Common Mistake The next position after 9 elements is the **left child of 15**. It is not the right child. For a 0-based array, the children of index i are:     textLeft child=2i+1     textRight child=2i+2     Here, 15 is at index 4:     textLeft child=2(4)+1=9     So 35 is initially placed at index 9$."
