    question: "A complete binary min-heap is made by including each integer in [1,1023] exactly once. The depth of a node in the heap is the length of the path from the root of the heap to that node. Thus, the root is at depth 0. The maximum depth at which integer 9 can appear is_____. .",
    image: null,
    options: [],
    answer: "8",
    explanation: "#  ## Logic  - A complete binary min-heap with n = 1023 nodes has a maximum depth of d_max = lfloor log_2(1023) rfloor = 9. - The heap contains all integers in [1, 1023] exactly once. - The min-heap property requires that every parent node is less than or equal to its children. Therefore, all ancestors of 9 must be ≤ 9. Objective  - Find the maximum depth d at which the integer 9 can appear while satisfying the min-heap property. Placement Strategy  - To maximize the depth of 9, place the smallest integers (1 to 8) in the shallowest levels (closest to the root). - Place 9 as far down as possible while ensuring its ancestors are ≤ 9. Calculation - The number of nodes ≤ 9 in the heap is 9 (integers 1 to 9). - Consider a path from the root to a node at depth d. This path has d + 1 nodes. - To place 9 at depth d, all nodes on the path from root to 9 must be less than or equal to 9. Therefore,  d + 1 ≤ 9, implying d ≤ 8. - Now, we establish that the maximum depth is indeed 8. - Place integers 1 to 8 on the path from the root to depth 8. Then, place integer 9 at depth 8. - All ancestors of 9 will be less than or equal to 9, satisfying the min-heap property along that path. - The remaining nodes at depth 8 and below can be filled in a manner that maintains the heap property. Conclusion  - The maximum depth at which 9 can appear is 8. Quick Tip Use the fact that 9 can only be a child of nodes 1–8; count levels until all such parents are exhausted."
