    id: "PYQ1216", year: 2021, subject: "Data Structures", topic: "Hashing",
    type: "MSQ", marks: 2,
    question: "Consider a dynamic hashing approach for 4-bit integer keys:  1. There is a main hash table of size 4.  2. The 2 least significant bits of a key are used to index into the main hash table.  3. Initially, all the main hash table entries are empty.  4. Whenever collisions occur, the set of keys corresponding to a main hash table entry is organized as a binary tree that grows on demand.  5. The first split uses the 3^textrd least significant bit to divide the keys into left and right subtrees.  6. Subsequent splits use the 4^textth least significant bit.  7. A split is performed only when a collision occurs.  The following figure shows the current state of the hash table.  Which of the following sequence of key insertions can produce the above state of the hash table? Assume that the keys are represented in decimal notation.",
    image: null,
    options: ["5,9,4,13,10,7", "9,5,10,6,7,1", "10,9,6,7,5,13", "9,5,13,6,10,14"],
    answer: "10,9,6,7,5,13",
    explanation: "Solution The hash table uses the **2 least significant bits (LSBs)** for the initial indexing. Whenever a collision occurs, the next higher-order bit is used to split the bucket. The binary representations of the keys are:  - 10 = 1010_2 - 9 = 1001_2 - 6 = 0110_2 - 7 = 0111_2 - 5 = 0101_2 - 13 = 1101_2 Insert 10 The 2 LSBs are 10. Hence, 10 is inserted into bucket 10. Insert 9 The 2 LSBs are 01. Hence, 9 is inserted into bucket 01. Insert 6 The 2 LSBs are 10, causing a collision with 10. Compare the 3rd LSB:  - 10 (1010): 3rd LSB =0 - 6 (0110): 3rd LSB =1 The bucket splits:  - Left child: 10 - Right child: 6 Insert 7 The 2 LSBs are 11. Hence, 7 is inserted into bucket 11. Insert 5 The 2 LSBs are 01, causing a collision with 9. Compare the 3rd LSB:  - 9 (1001): 3rd LSB =0 - 5 (0101): 3rd LSB =1 The bucket splits:  - Left child: 9 - Right child: 5 Insert 13 The 2 LSBs are 01. Using the 3rd LSB,  13 (1101) moves to the right child, where it collides with 5. Compare the 4th LSB:  - 5 (0101): 4th LSB =0 - 13 (1101): 4th LSB =1 The right child splits:  - Left child: 5 - Right child: 13 The final hash table matches the given structure. Final Answer boxed10, 9, 6, 7, 5, 13  ## 💡 Quick Tip In dynamic (extendible) hashing, resolve collisions by examining one additional bit at each split. Continue until the colliding keys are separated.  ## ⚠️ Common Mistake Do not compare all bits of the key during insertion. At each collision, use **only the next significant bit** required for that particular split."
