    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "A company maintains records of sales made by its salespersons and pays them commission based on each individual's total sales made in a year. This data is maintained in a table with following schema: salesinfo = (salespersonid, totalsales, commission) In a certain year, due to better business results, the company decides to further reward its salespersons by enhancing the commission paid to them as per the following formula: If commission ≤ 50000, enhance it by 2% If 50000 < commission ≤ 100000, enhance it by 4% If commission > 100000, enhance it by 6% The IT staff has written three different SQL scripts to calculate enhancement for each slab, each of these scripts is to run as a separate transaction as follows: ```sql T1 Update salesinfo Set commission = commission * 1.02 Where commission <= 50000; ``` ```sql T2 Update salesinfo Set commission = commission * 1.04 Where commission > 50000 and commission <= 100000; ``` ```sql T3 Update salesinfo Set commission = commission * 1.06 Where commission > 100000; ``` Which of the following options of running these transactions will update the commission of all salespersons correctly",
    image: null,
    options: ["Execute T1 followed by T2 followed by T3", "Execute T2, followed by T3; T1 running concurrently throughout", "Execute T3 followed by T2; T1 running concurrently throughout", "Execute T3 followed by T2 followed by T1"],
    answer: "Execute T1 followed by T2 followed by T3",
    explanation: "Correct Order - T3; commission > 100000, enhance by 6%. - T2; 50000 < commission <= 100000, enhance by 4%. - T1; commission <= 50000, enhance by 2%.  - Descending order avoids reclassification. - Example: commission = 49999. T1: 49999 * 1.02 = 50998.98. Incorrect if T1 runs first. Quick Tip When updating values based on ranges, processing from the highest range downwards often prevents unintended re-categorization."
