    question: "Consider two well-formed formulas in prepositional logic. F1:   PRightarrow ¬ P F2:  ( PRightarrow ¬ P)∨ ( ¬ PRightarrow P) Which of the following statements is correct?",
    image: null,
    options: ["F1 is satisfiable, F2 is valid", "F1 unsatisfiable, F2 is satisfiable", "F1 is unsatisfiable, F2 is valid", "F1 and F2 are both satisfiable"],
    answer: "F1 is satisfiable, F2 is valid",
    explanation: "Derivation Let's evaluate both well-formed formulas using the implication equivalence rule: A to B equiv ¬ A lor B. **Evaluate F1:** P to ¬ P   F1 = ¬ P lor ¬ P   F1 = ¬ P    Is F1 satisfiable? A formula is satisfiable if there is at least one truth assignment that makes it True. If we set P to False, then \\neg P becomes True. Since it can be made True, F1 is **satisfiable**. **Evaluate F2:** (P \\to \\neg P) \\lor (\\neg P \\to P) Using the previous expansion for the first block, and expanding the second block:    F2 = (¬ P lor ¬ P) lor (¬ (¬ P) lor P)   F2 = (\\neg P) \\lor (P \\lor P)   F2 = \\neg P \\lor P  $  This translates to \"False OR True\", which is always True regardless of the state of P.  Because it is always True for all possible assignments, F2 is a tautology, meaning it is **valid**. Therefore, F1 is satisfiable and F2 is valid. Common Trap Aspirants frequently confuse \"satisfiable\" with \"valid\".  - Valid = True for ALL inputs (Tautology). - Satisfiable = True for AT LEAST ONE input. - Unsatisfiable = False for ALL inputs (Contradiction).  Seeing P \\to \\neg P often makes students jump to \"Contradiction\" (Unsatisfiable) because it sounds paradoxical, forgetting that a False premise makes an implication True. AIR-1 Insight Implications with the same variable on both sides collapse instantly: - X \\to X = \\text{True} (Valid) - X \\to \\neg X = \\neg X (Satisfiable) - \\neg X \\to X = X$ (Satisfiable) Knowing these three fast-collapse rules eliminates the need for any truth table sketching. Quick Tip For propositional logic, quickly test satisfiability/validity by substituting true/false for variables."
