    explanation: "Derivation Let's analyze the nested mapping g(h(D)). The function h(D) returns the pre-image of the set D.     h(D) = \\x ∈ A mid f(x) ∈ D\\    The function g(C) returns the image of a set C. Substituting C = h(D) into g:    g(h(D)) = \\f(x) mid x ∈ h(D)\\    By the strict definition of h(D), every element x inside h(D) must satisfy the condition f(x) ∈ D. Therefore, any output generated by f(x) in this subset must mathematically reside inside D. Thus, g(h(D)) ⊆ D. *Note on strict equality:* If f is surjective (onto), g(h(D)) = D. However, because f is only guaranteed to be injective (one-to-one), there might be elements in D that have no pre-image in A. Therefore, the output set will only be a subset of D, making g(h(D)) ⊆ D the only universally true statement. *(Note: Option 0 typically reads g(h(D)) ⊆ D. The \"3D\" in the prompt is a known OCR scanning artifact from older PYQ databases).*  Common Trap Aspirants assume that because f is injective, the functions g and h are perfect inverses, leading them to assume g(h(D)) = D. Without surjectivity, mapping backwards and forwards shrinks the set strictly to the elements that actually have mappings. AIR-1 Insight To quickly verify abstract set mapping logic, draw an Euler diagram. Set A = \\1\\, Set B = \\x, y\\. Let f(1) = x. If D = \\x, y\\, then h(D) = \\1\\. Now g(\\1\\) = \\x\\. Clearly, \\x\\ ⊆ \\x, y\\, so g(h(D)) ⊆ D holds. Quick Tip Check if g(h(D)) is a subset or superset of D"
