    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "Let a(x, y), b(x, y,) and c(x, y) be three statements with variables x and y chosen from some universe. Consider the following statement:  (∃ x)(∀ y)[(a(x, y) ∧ b(x, y)) ∧ ¬ c(x, y)] Which one of the following is its equivalent?",
    image: null,
    options: ["(∀ x)(∃ y)[(a(x, y) ∨ b(x, y)) to c(x, y)]", "(∃ x)(∀ y)[(a(x, y) ∨ b(x, y)) ∧¬ c(x, y)]", "¬ (∀ x)(∃ y)[(a(x, y) ∧ b(x, y)) to c(x, y)]", "¬ (∀ x)(∃ y)[(a(x, y) ∨ b(x, y)) to c(x, y)]"],
    answer: "¬ (∀ x)(∃ y)[(a(x, y) ∧ b(x, y)) to c(x, y)]",
    explanation: "Derivation We must find the logical equivalent of the given formula: (∃ x)(∀ y) [ (a(x,y) land b(x,y)) land ¬ c(x,y) ]    To simplify, let P represent the bundled condition (a(x,y) land b(x,y)). The expression becomes:    ∃ x ∀ y [ P land ¬ c ]    Recall the standard definition of logical implication: P to c equiv ¬ P lor c. If we negate the implication, we get:    ¬ (P to c) equiv ¬ (¬ P lor c) equiv P land ¬ c    We can substitute this equivalence back into our quantified expression:    ∃ x ∀ y [ ¬ (P to c) ]    Now, we pull the negation operator (¬) outside the quantifiers. When negation passes through quantifiers, it flips them (De Morgan's Laws for Predicate Logic: ∀ becomes ∃, and ∃ becomes ∀):    ∃ x ∀ y [ ¬ (dots) ] equiv ¬ ∀ x ∃ y [ dots ]    Applying this gives the final formula:    ¬ (∀ x)(∃ y) [ P to c ]    Substituting P back yields:    ¬ (∀ x)(∃ y) [ (a(x,y) land b(x,y)) to c(x,y) ]    This derivation perfectly matches Option 2. Common Trap Aspirants often try to push negations outward without flipping the quantifiers, or they mistakenly apply De Morgan's law to the P term, changing the (a land b) into (a lor b). Because the implication P to c encapsulates P completely, the internal land gate remains untouched. AIR-1 Insight When faced with A land ¬ B, always recognize it instantly as the negated implication ¬(A to B). This is a universal design pattern in GATE logic questions used to hide conditional statements inside conjunction blocks. Quick Tip Use the fact that ¬ (∀ x) equiv (∃ x) ¬ and ¬ (∃ x) equiv (∀ x) ¬."
