    explanation: "Derivation Let P(E) be the total probability of all even faces, and P(O) be the total probability of all odd faces. Given: P(O) = 0.9 P(E). Since the sum of all probabilities must equal 1:    P(O) + P(E) = 1 implies 1.9 P(E) = 1 implies P(E) = frac11.9 = frac1019    We are told all even faces (2, 4, 6) have the same probability:    P(2) = P(4) = P(6) = frac10/193 = frac1057    We are given a conditional probability constraint: P(texteven mid >3) = 0.75. By definition of conditional probability:    P(texteven mid >3) = fracP(texteven ∩ >3)P(>3)    The even faces strictly greater than 3 are 4 and 6. Therefore:    P(texteven ∩ >3) = P(4) + P(6) = frac1057 + frac1057 = frac2057    Substitute this into the conditional equation: 0.75 = frac20/57P(>3)   P(>3) = frac20/573/4 = frac2057 × frac43 = frac80171    Converting the fraction to a decimal:    P(>3) approx 0.4678    Rounding to three decimal places yields 0.468, which perfectly matches Option B. Common Trap Aspirants try to explicitly solve for the individual probabilities of the odd faces (1, 3, and 5) by setting up overly complex systems of equations. The cleverness of Bayes' theorem is that P(>3) acts as an encapsulated denominator block that can be algebraically isolated without ever needing to know P(5). AIR-1 Insight Always write down the expanded Bayes formula definition first: P(A \\mid B) = P(A \\cap B) / P(B)$. In 90% of tricky GATE probability questions, the numerator is a trivial lookup, and the complex unknown is isolated elegantly by a single division. Quick Tip Set up equations using total probability =1 and conditional probability; solve linear system quickly."
