    explanation: "Step 1: Check Option A  ∀ x∃ yR(x,y)leftrightarrow∃ y∀ xR(x,y)  These two statements are not equivalent.  The left side means:  \"For every x, there exists some y that may depend on x.\"  The right side means:  \"There exists one particular y that works for every x.\"  The existential variable cannot generally be moved across the universal quantifier.  Therefore, A is not a tautology.  Step 2: Check Option B  (∀ x[∃ yR(x,y)→ S(x,y)])→∀ x∃ yS(x,y)  The premise does not guarantee that S(x,y) is true for some y for every x.  So we can construct an interpretation in which the premise is true but the conclusion is false.  Therefore, B is not a tautology.  Also, as written, notice that y occurring in S(x,y) inside the antecedent is not bound by the ∃ y when that quantifier is restricted to R(x,y). This is why the \"well-formed formula\" wording is itself relevant here.  Step 3: Check Option C  Consider:  P(x,y)→ R(x,y)  Using the implication equivalence:  P(x,y)→ R(x,y)equiv¬ P(x,y)lor R(x,y)  Therefore:  ∀ x∃ y(P(x,y)→ R(x,y))  equiv∀ x∃ y(¬ P(x,y)lor R(x,y))  The quantifiers are unchanged.  Thus Option C becomes:  Aleftrightarrow A  which is always true.  Hence C is a tautology.  Step 4: Important distinction about Option D  Option D is:  ∀ x∀ yP(x,y)→∀ x∀ yP(y,x)  This formula is actually logically valid: if P is true for every ordered pair (x,y), then it is certainly true for the swapped pair (y,x).  However, in first-order logic, \"tautology\" and \"valid formula\" are treated as different concepts in this question.  A tautology follows from the logical structure/connectives themselves, whereas validity concerns truth under every interpretation.  Therefore, D is not the intended answer.  ## Official Answer Check  accepted answer is **C**, with exactly the same reasoning: P→ Qequiv¬ Plor Q, while the quantifiers remain unchanged.   ## Final Answer  boxedtextC  ## 💡 Quick Tip  For this question, the fastest route is simply:  P→ Qequiv¬ Plor Q  Therefore:  [∀ x∃ y(P→ R)]leftrightarrow[∀ x∃ y(¬ Plor R)]  is immediately a tautology.  So mark:  boxedtextC"
