    explanation: "Derivation We are given that the logical expression X: (p to q) to r is a contradiction (always evaluates to False). In propositional logic, an implication A to B is False under exactly one condition: when the antecedent A is True and the consequent B is False. Therefore, for (p to q) to r to evaluate to False, it must be that: 1. p to q is True. 2. r is False. *(Note: For this to be a strict contradiction rather than a contingency, the variables must be fixed propositions with these specific static truth values in the context of the problem).*  Now, we must evaluate the target expression: (r to p) to q. Substitute the known value of r = textFalse into the expression: (textFalse to p) to q    By the rules of implication, a False antecedent makes the implication universally True (vacuous truth). So, (textFalse to p) evaluates to True, regardless of p. The expression now simplifies to:    textTrue to q    The truth value of (textTrue to q) depends entirely on q.  - If q is True, then textTrue to textTrue is True. - If q is False, then textTrue to textFalse is False. Thus, the expression evaluates strictly to the value of q. It is \"always TRUE when q is TRUE\". Common Trap Aspirants often see the word \"contradiction\" and try to build a massive 8-row truth table for all possible states of p, q, r, wasting 5 minutes. The premise forces specific states; isolating r = textFalse short-circuits the entire table. AIR-1 Insight Memorize the short-circuit rules of implication: - F to X = T (Vacuous Truth) - X to T = T  - T to X = X (Identity) Because we found r=F, the first block (r to p) instantly collapses to T, activating the identity rule T to q = q. Quick Tip Use truth‑table elimination: test only the rows that make (p→q)→r false to deduce the second expression’s behavior."
