    explanation: "#  ## Logic - Let S represent the signal transmitted by the sender, and R represent the signal received by the receiver. - The possible signals are H and L. - We are given the following probabilities:   - Prior probabilities: P(S=H) = 0.1 and P(S=L) = 0.9.   - Conditional probabilities: P(R=L | S=H) = 0.7, implying P(R=H | S=H) = 1 - 0.7 = 0.3, and P(R=H | S=L) = 0.1.  Calculation of P(R=H) - We use the law of total probability to find the probability of receiving H:    P(R=H) = P(R=H | S=H)P(S=H) + P(R=H | S=L)P(S=L)   P(R=H) = (0.3)(0.1) + (0.1)(0.9) = 0.03 + 0.09 = 0.12  Calculation of P(S=H | R=H) - We want to find the probability that the transmitted signal was H given that the received signal was H, i.e., P(S=H | R=H). - Using Bayes' Theorem:    P(S=H | R=H) = fracP(R=H | S=H)P(S=H)P(R=H)   P(S=H | R=H) = frac(0.3)(0.1)0.12 = frac0.030.12 = 0.25    Therefore, if the received signal is H, the probability that the transmitted signal was H$ is 0.25. Quick Tip No quick tip available"
