    id: "PYQ2105", year: 2024, subject: "Engineering Mathematics", topic: "Calculus",
    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "The value of the line integral ∈t_P^Q(z^2 d x+3 y^2 d y+2 x z d z) along the straight line joining the points P(1,1,2) and Q(2,3,1) is",
    image: null,
    options: ["20", "24", "29", "-5"],
    answer: "-5",
    explanation: "Logic  The problem requires evaluating the line integral ∈t_P^Q (z^2 dx + 3y^2 dy + 2xz dz) along the straight line joining the points P(1,1,2) and Q(2,3,1).  - **Parametrization of the Line Segment:**   - The direction vector vecPQ is given by Q - P = beginpmatrix 2 - 1 \\ 3 - 1 \\ 1 - 2 endpmatrix = beginpmatrix 1 \\ 2 \\ -1 endpmatrix.   - The line segment can be parametrized as vecr(t) = P + t · vecPQ = beginpmatrix 1 + t \\ 1 + 2t \\ 2 - t \\ endpmatrix, where t ∈ [0, 1].   - Thus, x(t) = 1 + t, y(t) = 1 + 2t, and z(t) = 2 - t.   - The differentials are dx = dt, dy = 2dt, and dz = -dt.  - **Substitution into the Line Integral:**   - Substitute the parametrization into the integral:     ∈t_0^1 [ (2 - t)^2 dt + 3(1 + 2t)^2 (2dt) + 2(1 + t)(2 - t)(-dt) ].   - Simplify the integrand:     - (2 - t)^2 = 4 - 4t + t^2     - 3(1 + 2t)^2 · 2 = 6(1 + 4t + 4t^2) = 6 + 24t + 24t^2     - 2(1 + t)(2 - t)(-1) = -2(2 + t - t^2) = -4 - 2t + 2t^2   - Combine the terms:     4 - 4t + t^2 + 6 + 24t + 24t^2 - 4 - 2t + 2t^2 = 6 + 18t + 27t^2.  - **Integration:**   - Evaluate the integral:     ∈t_0^1 (6 + 18t + 27t^2) dt = [ 6t + 9t^2 + 9t^3 ]_0^1 = 6(1) + 9(1)^2 + 9(1)^3 = 6 + 9 + 9 = 24.  Therefore, the value of the line integral is 24.  Quick Tip No quick tip available"
