    answer: "(4yz^3+2xy^2)hati+2x^2zhatj-2y^2zhatk",
    explanation: "Derivation The curl of a vector field vecF = F_xhati + F_yhatj + F_zhatk is defined by the determinant of the formal matrix:    nabla × vecF = beginvmatrix hati & hatj & hatk \\ fracpartialpartial x & fracpartialpartial y & fracpartialpartial z \\ F_x & F_y & F_z endvmatrix     Given vecF = x^2z^2hati - 2xy^2zhatj + 2y^2z^3hatk. Let us compute the determinant expansion component by component: **1. The hati component:**    ( fracpartial F_zpartial y - fracpartial F_ypartial z ) = fracpartialpartial y(2y^2z^3) - fracpartialpartial z(-2xy^2z)  = 4yz^3 - (-2xy^2) = 4yz^3 + 2xy^2 **2. The hatj component:** *Warning: Note the standard negative sign associated with the middle term of a determinant expansion.*  - ( fracpartial F_zpartial x - fracpartial F_xpartial z ) = - [ fracpartialpartial x(2y^2z^3) - fracpartialpartial z(x^2z^2) ]   = - [ 0 - 2x^2z ] = + 2x^2z **3. The hatk component:**    ( fracpartial F_ypartial x - fracpartial F_xpartial y ) = fracpartialpartial x(-2xy^2z) - fracpartialpartial y(x^2z^2)  = -2y^2z - 0 = -2y^2z    Reassembling the components yields the final vector:    nabla × vecF = (4yz^3 + 2xy^2)hati + 2x^2zhatj - 2y^2zhatk Common Trap The most common error occurs in the \\hat{j} component. Students frequently forget the negative multiplier applied to the j-minor determinant in the cross product expansion, yielding a sign error (-2x^2z\\hat{j} instead of +2x^2z\\hat{j}). Option B is deliberately planted to trap this mistake. AIR-1 Insight To avoid the \\hat{j} sign trap, always write the curl formula strictly as positive cyclic permutations: \\hat{i}(\\partial_y F_z - \\partial_z F_y) + \\hat{j}(\\partial_z F_x - \\partial_x F_z) + \\hat{k}(\\partial_x F_y - \\partial_y F_x). Notice the reverse order (\\partial_z F_x - \\partial_x F_z) in the \\hat{j} term inherently bakes in the negative sign, removing the mental overhead during exam pressure. Quick Tip Remember curl = (\\partial F_z/\\partial y - \\partial F_y/\\partial z)\\hat{i} + (\\partial F_x/\\partial z - \\partial F_z/\\partial x)\\hat{j} + (\\partial F_y/\\partial x - \\partial F_x/\\partial y)\\hat{k}$."
