    explanation: "Derivation We must evaluate the definite integral I = ∈t_1^e sqrtx ln(x) dx. This integration requires the method of Integration by Parts: ∈t u   dv = uv - ∈t v   du. Following the LIATE rule (Logarithmic, Inverse, Algebraic, Trigonometric, Exponential), we set the logarithmic function as u.  **Step 1: Set up parts** * Let u = ln(x) implies du = frac1x dx * Let dv = x^1/2 dx implies v = ∈t x^1/2 dx = frac23 x^3/2  **Step 2: Apply Integration by Parts**  ∈t x^1/2 ln(x) dx = ( ln(x) · frac23 x^3/2 ) - ∈t ( frac23 x^3/2 · frac1x ) dx   = frac23 x^3/2 ln(x) - frac23 ∈t x^1/2 dx   = frac23 x^3/2 ln(x) - frac23 ( frac23 x^3/2 )   = frac23 x^3/2 ln(x) - frac49 x^3/2   **Step 3: Evaluate at the limits [1, e]** Evaluate the upper limit (e):  F(e) = frac23 e^3/2 ln(e) - frac49 e^3/2 = frac23 e^3/2 (1) - frac49 e^3/2 = e^3/2 ( frac69 - frac49 ) = frac29 e^3/2   Evaluate the lower limit (1):  F(1) = frac23 (1)^3/2 ln(1) - frac49 (1)^3/2 = 0 - frac49 = -frac49   Final Definite Integral:  I = F(e) - F(1) = frac29 e^3/2 - ( -frac49 ) = frac29 e^3/2 + frac49   Common Trap A common arithmetic trap is improperly handling the negative signs when subtracting the lower limit. F(1) yields a negative value (-frac49), so subtracting it equates to adding frac49. Option B is an explicit distractor designed to catch this oversight.  AIR-1 Insight When managing definite integrals with x=1 as a lower limit, logarithmic terms immediately zero out because ln(1) = 0. This allows you to rapidly skip computations and purely focus on the v · du term for the lower boundary evaluation, saving critical time.  Quick Tip Remember ∫x^{n}dx = x^{n+1}/(n+1); after parts, the remaining integral is a simple power of x."
