    explanation: "Derivation We are given the parametric equations of a cycloid curve:    x = a(θ + sin θ)   y = a(1 - cos θ)    To find the derivative \\frac{dy}{dx}, we must compute the derivatives of x and y with respect to the parameter \\theta, and apply the chain rule logic \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy/d\\theta}{dx/d\\theta}. First, differentiate y with respect to \\theta:    fracdydθ = a(0 - (-sin θ)) = a sin θ    Next, differentiate x with respect to \\theta:    fracdxdθ = a(1 + cos θ)    Divide to find \\frac{dy}{dx}:    fracdydx = fraca sin θa(1 + cos θ) = fracsin θ1 + cos θ    To simplify this to match the options, utilize the trigonometric half-angle identities: 1. \\sin \\theta = 2 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) 2. 1 + \\cos \\theta = 2 \\cos^2\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) Substitute these identities back into the fraction:    fracdydx = frac2 sin(fracθ2) cos(fracθ2)2 cos^2(fracθ2)    Cancel out the common 2 \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) terms from the numerator and denominator:    fracdydx = fracsin(fracθ2)cos(fracθ2) = tan(fracθ2) Common Trap Students often incorrectly map the fundamental identity 1 - \\cos \\theta = 2\\sin^2(\\frac{\\theta}{2}) when they actually need 1 + \\cos \\theta = 2\\cos^2(\\frac{\\theta}{2}). Swapping these leads to \\cot(\\theta/2) instead of \\tan(\\theta/2). AIR-1 Insight This specific parametric curve is a generic cycloid (the path traced by a point on a rolling wheel). A famous geometric property of the standard cycloid is that the slope of its tangent line at any parameter \\theta is exactly \\tan(\\theta/2) or \\cot(\\theta/2)$ depending on the initial orientation phase. Familiarity with cycloid kinematics makes this a visual check. Quick Tip Use tan(θ/2)=sinθ/(1+cosθ) to simplify the ratio."
