    id: "PYQ2710", year: 2025, subject: "General Aptitude", topic: "Analytical Aptitude",
    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "Consider the relationships among P, Q, R, S, and T: <ul><li>P is the brother of Q.</li><li>S is the daughter of Q.</li><li>T is the sister of S.</li><li>R is the mother of Q.</li></ul>The following statements are made based on the relationships given above. 1. R is the grandmother of S. 2. P is the uncle of S and T. 3. R has only one son. 4. Q has only one daughter. Which one of the following options is <b>CORRECT</b>?",
    image: null,
    options: ["Both (1) and (2) are true.", "Both (1) and (3) are true.", "Only (3) is true.", "Only (4) is true."],
    answer: "Both (1) and (2) are true.",
    explanation: "Detailed Explanation  **Core Concept: Family Relationships and Logical Deduction** This problem tests your ability to interpret and deduce family relationships based on given statements. The key is to systematically map out the relationships and verify each statement against the constructed family tree. Step-by-Step Solution **1. Construct the Family Tree** We start by translating the given relationships into a family tree structure.  - **Given Relationships:**   1. **P is the brother of Q.**      - This means P and Q are siblings, and P is male.   2. **S is the daughter of Q.**      - Q is the parent of S, and S is female.   3. **T is the sister of S.**      - T and S are siblings, and T is female.   4. **R is the mother of Q.**      - R is the parent of Q, and R is female.  - **Derived Relationships:**   - Since R is the mother of Q, and P is the brother of Q, R is also the mother of P.   - S and T are children of Q (since T is the sister of S, and S is the daughter of Q).   - P is the uncle of S and T (since P is the brother of Q, and Q is the parent of S and T).   - R is the grandmother of S and T (since R is the mother of Q, and Q is the parent of S and T).  **2. Visual Representation of the Family Tree** For clarity, here is a simple representation:  ```         R (Female)        /    \\ \\\\      P (M)  Q (Gender not specified)             |        ---------       /          \\ \\\\     S (F)      T (F) ```  - **Key Notes:**   - The genders of Q and R are not explicitly stated beyond what is given (R is female, P is male, S and T are female).   - Q could be male or female, but since P is the brother of Q, and no other siblings are mentioned, Q's gender does not affect the given statements.  **3. Evaluate Each Statement**  **Statement 1: R is the grandmother of S.** - R is the mother of Q. - Q is the parent of S. - Therefore, R is the grandmother of S. - **Conclusion:** True.  **Statement 2: P is the uncle of S and T.** - P is the brother of Q. - Q is the parent of S and T. - Therefore, P is the uncle of S and T. - **Conclusion:** True.  **Statement 3: R has only one son.** - R has at least two children: P (male) and Q (gender not specified). - If Q is female, then P is R's only son. - If Q is male, then R has two sons (P and Q). - The problem does not specify Q's gender, so we cannot definitively say R has only one son. - **Conclusion:** False (cannot be confirmed as true).  **Statement 4: Q has only one daughter.** - Q has two daughters: S and T. - Therefore, Q does not have only one daughter. - **Conclusion:** False.  **4. Match the Statements with the Options** - **Option A:** Both (1) and (2) are true.   - This matches our evaluation (1 and 2 are true). - **Option B:** Both (1) and (3) are true.   - (3) is false, so this is incorrect. - **Option C:** Only (3) is true.   - (3) is false, so this is incorrect. - **Option D:** Only (4) is true.   - (4) is false, so this is incorrect.  **5. Final Answer** The correct option is **A**.  💡 Quick Tip **Key Takeaway:** - Always draw a family tree to visualize relationships. - Pay attention to gender specifications (e.g., \"brother\" implies male, \"sister\" implies female). - Do not assume genders unless explicitly stated (e.g., Q's gender is not given, so avoid assumptions about R's number of sons).  ⚠️ Common Mistake **Pitfall:** - Assuming Q is male because P is the brother of Q. This is incorrect because Q could be female (e.g., P and Q could be brother and sister). - Overlooking that T is also a daughter of Q, which makes statement 4 false."
