    id: "PYQ2835", year: 2006, subject: "Operating Systems", topic: "Memory Management",
    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "For each of the four processes P_1, P_2, P_3, and P_4 . The total size in kilobytes (KB) and the number of segments are given below. beginarray|c|c|c|hline textbfProcess & textbfTotal size (∈ KB) & textbfNumber of segments \\\\hline textP_1 & text195& text4\\\\hline textP_2 & text254 & text5\\\\hline textP_3 & text45& text3 \\\\hline textP_4 & text364& text8 \\\\hline endarray The page size is 1 KB. The size of an entry in the page table is 4 bytes. The size of an entry in the segment table is 8 bytes. The maximum size of a segment is 256 KB. The paging method for memory management uses two-level paging, and its storage overhead is P. The storage overhead for the segmentation method is S. The storage overhead for the segmentation and paging method is T. What is the relation among the overheads for the different methods of memory management in the concurrent execution of the above four processes?",
    image: null,
    options: ["P  <  S  <  T", "S  <  P  <  T", "S  <  T  <  P", "T < S  <  P"],
    answer: "S  <  P  <  T",
    explanation: "Logic  - We are given the size of four processes, P_1, P_2, P_3, and P_4, along with the number of segments each process contains.  The goal is to calculate and compare the storage overhead for paging (P), segmentation (S), and a combined segmentation and paging approach (T).  1. Paging Overhead (P): Two-Level Paging  - Each process uses two-level paging. The page size is 1 KB, which is 1024 bytes. Each page table entry is 4 bytes. - Since the page size is 1 KB, the number of pages needed for a process is equal to its size in KB. - The page table size for a process is then the number of pages multiplied by the size of each page table entry (4 bytes).  Calculations:  - P_1: Size = 195 KB, Pages = 195, Page table size = 195 	imes 4 = 780 bytes. - P_2: Size = 254 KB, Pages = 254, Page table size = 254 	imes 4 = 1016 bytes. - P_3: Size = 45 KB, Pages = 45, Page table size = 45 	imes 4 = 180 bytes. - P_4: Size = 364 KB, Pages = 364, Page table size = 364 	imes 4 = 1456 bytes.  Total Paging Overhead (P):  - P = 780 + 1016 + 180 + 1456 = 3432 bytes.  P = 3432 text bytes  2. Segmentation Overhead (S): Pure Segmentation  - Each process has its own segment table.  Each segment table entry is 8 bytes. - The segment table size for a process is the number of segments multiplied by the size of each segment table entry.  Calculations:  - P_1: Segments = 4, Segment table size = 4 	imes 8 = 32 bytes. - P_2: Segments = 5, Segment table size = 5 	imes 8 = 40 bytes. - P_3: Segments = 3, Segment table size = 3 	imes 8 = 24 bytes. - P_4: Segments = 8, Segment table size = 8 	imes 8 = 64 bytes.  Total Segmentation Overhead (S):  - S = 32 + 40 + 24 + 64 = 160 bytes.  S = 160 text bytes  3. Combined Overhead (T): Segmentation + Paging  - In this approach, each segment is further divided into pages. Each process has a segment table (8 bytes per entry) and a page table for each segment (4 bytes per entry). - First, calculate the number of pages per segment for each process, rounding up to the nearest whole number since we can't have partial pages.  Calculations:  - P_1: Size = 195 KB, Segments = 4, Pages/Segment = lceil frac1954 rceil = 49 pages/segment. - P_2: Size = 254 KB, Segments = 5, Pages/Segment = lceil frac2545 rceil = 51 pages/segment. - P_3: Size = 45 KB, Segments = 3, Pages/Segment = lceil frac453 rceil = 15 pages/segment. - P_4: Size = 364 KB, Segments = 8, Pages/Segment = lceil frac3648 rceil = 46 pages/segment.  - The size of the page table for a segment is the number of pages per segment multiplied by 4 bytes. - Then, we multiply this by the number of segments per process to get total paging overhead  Total paging overhead per process:  - P_1: 4 × (49 × 4) = 784 bytes - P_2: 5 × (51 × 4) = 1020 bytes - P_3: 3 × (15 × 4) = 180 bytes - P_4: 8 × (46 × 4) = 1472 bytes  Total Paging Overhead for Segmentation + Paging:  - T_textpaging = 784 + 1020 + 180 + 1472 = 3456 bytes  Total Combined Overhead (T):  -  T = S + T_textpaging = 160 + 3456 = 3616 bytes  T = 3616 text bytes  4. Overhead Comparison  - S = 160 bytes - P = 3432 bytes - T = 3616 bytes  Therefore, the relation among the overheads is:  S < P < T   Quick Tip Compare the page table and segment table sizes to eliminate options"
