    explanation: "Problem Setup  - Page size: 8 text KB = 8 	imes 2^10 = 2^13 text bytes. - Physical address space: 32 text bits, implying a physical memory size of 2^32 text bytes. - Maximum page table size: 24 text MB = 24 	imes 2^20 text bytes. - Each page table entry (PTE) contains:   - Valid bit: 1 text bit.   - Dirty bit: 1 text bit.   - Permission bits: 3 text bits.   - Physical page number (translation): x text bits.  Determining Physical Page Number Bits (x)  - The number of physical pages is the total physical memory size divided by the page size:  frac2^32 text bytes2^13 text bytes/page = 2^19 text pages   -  Therefore, the physical page number requires x bits, where 2^x = 2^19. Thus, x = 19 text bits.  Calculating PTE Size  - The total size of each PTE is the sum of the sizes of all its components:  1 text bit + 1 text bit + 3 text bits + 19 text bits = 24 text bits   - Converting this to bytes: frac24 text bits8 text bits/byte = 3 text bytes.  Finding the Number of Page Table Entries (PTEs)  - The maximum page table size is 24 text MB = 24 	imes 2^20 text bytes. - The number of PTEs is the page table size divided by the size of each PTE:  frac24 	imes 2^20 text bytes3 text bytes/PTE = 8 	imes 2^20 text PTEs = 2^3 	imes 2^20 = 2^23 text PTEs   Determining Virtual Address Space Size  -  The number of virtual pages is equal to the number of PTEs, which is 2^23. -  The virtual address space size is the number of virtual pages multiplied by the page size:  2^23 text pages 	imes 2^13 text bytes/page = 2^36 text bytes   Calculating Virtual Address Length  -  The virtual address length is the number of bits required to address the entire virtual address space. Since the virtual address space is 2^36 text bytes, the virtual address length is:  log_2(2^36) = 36 text bits   Therefore, the length of the virtual address supported by the system is 36 bits.  Quick Tip Use page size to get offset bits; PTE count = table size / PTE size."
