    explanation: "System Configuration - Number of physical page frames: m = 10 - Number of distinct virtual pages: n = 20 (a_1, a_2, ..., a_20) - Access sequence: (a_1, a_2, ..., a_20, a_1, a_2, ..., a_20) Optimal Page Replacement (MIN) - **Initial Loading:** The first ten page requests (a_1 to a_10) cause page faults. The physical frames now contain pages a_1 to a_10. Number of page faults = 10. - **Subsequent Accesses (a_11 to a_20):** Each new page a_i (for 11 ≤ i ≤ 20) replaces the page that will not be used for the longest time in the future. Since the sequence repeats after a_20, the optimal policy retains pages a_1 to a_10 (as they are reused immediately in the next cycle). - No additional page faults occur in the second cycle because all accessed pages are already in memory. - **Total Page Faults (Optimal):** 10 Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) Page Replacement - **Initial Loading:** The first ten page requests (a_1 to a_10) cause page faults. The physical frames now contain pages a_1 to a_10. Number of page faults = 10. - **Subsequent Accesses (a_11 to a_20):** LIFO replaces the most recently loaded page. Each new page a_i (for 11 ≤ i ≤ 20) replaces a_i-1, resulting in frames containing a_11 to a_20 after this phase. Page faults in this phase: 10. - **Second Cycle (a_1 to a_20 again):** The first access to a_1 causes a page fault, replacing a_20 (most recent). This pattern continues: every access to a_1 to a_10 causes a page fault, replacing a_20 down to a_11 sequentially. Page faults in this phase: 10. - **Total Page Faults (LIFO):** 10 + 10 + 10 = 30 Difference in Page Faults - **Difference:** 30 - 10 = 20. Quick Tip No quick tip available"
