    question: "Consider a computer system with 40-bit virtual addressing and page size of sixteen kilobytes. If the computer system has a one level page table per process and each page table entry requires 48 bits, then the size of the per-process page table is _________ mega bytes.",
    image: null,
    options: [],
    answer: "384",
    explanation: "Virtual Address Space and Page Size  - The virtual address space is determined by the number of virtual address bits. Given a 40-bit virtual address, the address space is 2^40 bytes. - The page size is given as 16 KB, which is equivalent to 16 	imes 1024 = 2^4 	imes 2^10 = 2^14 bytes.  Number of Pages  The number of pages in the virtual address space can be calculated by dividing the total address space by the page size:  textNumber of pages = fractextVirtual address spacetextPage size = frac2^402^14 = 2^40-14 = 2^26  Thus, the number of pages is 2^26.  Page Table Entry Size  The size of each page table entry (PTE) is given as 48 bits. To convert this to bytes:  textPTE size = 48 text bits = frac488 text bytes = 6 text bytes  Total Page Table Size  The total page table size is the product of the number of pages and the size of each page table entry:  textPage table size = textNumber of pages × textPTE size = 2^26 × 6 text bytes  Since 2^26 = 2^6 	imes 2^20 = 64 	imes 2^20, we have:  textPage table size = 64 × 2^20 × 6 text bytes = 384 × 2^20 text bytes  Conversion to Megabytes  To convert the page table size to megabytes, we divide by 2^20 (since 1 MB = 2^20 bytes):  textPage table size in MB = frac384 × 2^20 text bytes2^20 text bytes/MB = 384 text MB  Therefore, the size of the per-process page table is 384 MB.  Quick Tip Check units: 48 bits = 6 bytes, 16 KB = 2¹⁴ bytes, so table size = (2³⁶ pages × 6 B) ÷ 10⁶ ≈ 384 MB."
