    id: "PYQ2966", year: 2021, subject: "Operating Systems", topic: "Thread",
    type: "MSQ", marks: 2,
    question: "Consider the following multi-threaded code segment (in a mix of C and pseudo-code), invoked by two processes P1 and P2, and each of the processes spawns two threads T1 and T2: ```c int x = 0;  // global Lock L1;    // global void foo() {     int y = 0;     Acquire(L1);     x = x + 1;     y = y + 1;     Release(L1);     printf(\"%d \", y); }  int main () {     pthread_t thread1, thread2;     pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, foo, NULL);     pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, foo, NULL);     pthread_join(thread1, NULL);     pthread_join(thread2, NULL);     printf(\"%d \", x); } ```Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?",
    image: null,
    options: ["Both P1 and P2 will print the value of x as 2.", "At least of P1 and P2 will print the value of x as 4.", "At least one of the threads will print the value of y as 2.", "Both T1 and T2, in both the processes, will print the value of y as 1."],
    answer: "Both P1 and P2 will print the value of x as 2., Both T1 and T2, in both the processes, will print the value of y as 1.",
    explanation: "Solution Let x be the shared variable and y be the local variable of each thread. Initially,  - x=0 - Each thread has its own local variable initialized as y=0. Each thread performs:  - x=x+1 - y=y+1 Since x is shared, its value is updated by all threads. Since y is local to each thread, every thread maintains its own copy. Now evaluate each statement.  ## (A) After both threads of process P_1 execute,  x=2 At this point, P_1 prints the value of x. Hence, this output is **possible**. Therefore, (A) is **correct**.  ## (B) The value  x=4 is obtained only after both processes complete execution. No individual process can print x=4 as its own output. Therefore, (B) is **incorrect**.  ## (C) Each thread starts with  y=0 and increments it exactly once. Thus,  y=1 for every thread. Hence, y=2 can never be printed. Therefore, (C) is **incorrect**.  ## (D) Since every thread has its own local copy of y, each thread prints  y=1 Therefore, (D) is **correct**. Final Answer **(A) and (D)**  ## 💡 Quick Tip Always distinguish between **shared** and **local** variables. Shared variables are visible to all threads, whereas each thread maintains its own copy of a local variable.  ## ⚠️ Common Mistake Do not assume that the local variable of one thread is shared with other threads. Each thread initializes and updates its own copy independently."
