    id: "PYQ2992", year: 2025, subject: "Operating Systems", topic: "File Systems",
    type: "NAT", marks: 2,
    question: "A disk of size 512M bytes is divided into blocks of 64K bytes. A file is stored in the disk using linked allocation. Each data block reserves 4 bytes to store the pointer to the next data block. The link part of the last data block contains a NULL pointer (also of 4 bytes). Suppose a file of 1M bytes needs to be stored in the disk. Assume, 1K=2^10, 1M=2^20 . The amount of space in bytes that will be wasted due to internal fragmentation is _________. (Answer in integer)  ```c #define K (1 << 10) #define M (1 << 20) #define BLOCK_SIZE (1 << 16) #define POINTER_SIZE 4 #define FILE_SIZE (1 << 20)  int main() {     // Calculate usable data space per block     int usable_data_space_per_block = BLOCK_SIZE - POINTER_SIZE;      // Calculate number of blocks required     int num_blocks = (FILE_SIZE + usable_data_space_per_block - 1) / usable_data_space_per_block;      // Calculate total data stored in full blocks     int total_data_in_full_blocks = num_blocks * usable_data_space_per_block;      // Calculate remaining data to be stored in the last block     int remaining_data_in_last_block = FILE_SIZE - total_data_in_full_blocks;      // Calculate wasted space in the last block     int wasted_space = BLOCK_SIZE - (remaining_data_in_last_block + POINTER_SIZE);      return 0; } ```",
    image: null,
    options: [],
    answer: "65468",
    explanation: "Problem Statement A disk of size 512M bytes is divided into blocks of 64K bytes. A file is stored on the disk using linked allocation. Each data block reserves 4 bytes to store the pointer to the next data block. The link part of the last data block contains a NULL pointer (also of 4 bytes). Suppose a file of 1M bytes needs to be stored on the disk. Assume 1K = 2^10 and 1M = 2^20. Determine the amount of space in bytes that will be wasted due to internal fragmentation. Given Parameters  - Disk size: 512M = 512 × 2^20 = 2^9 × 2^20 = 2^29 bytes - Block size: 64K = 64 × 2^10 = 2^6 × 2^10 = 2^16 = 65536 bytes - File size: 1M = 2^20 = 1048576 bytes - Pointer size per block: 4 bytes - NULL pointer size in the last block: 4 bytes  Logic  - **Usable data space per block:** Each block reserves 4 bytes for the pointer, therefore, the usable data space per block is: 2^16 - 4 = 65536 - 4 = 65532 text bytes  - **Number of blocks required (n)**: Calculate the number of blocks needed to store the file:    n = lceil fractextFile sizetextUsable data space per block rceil = lceil frac104857665532 rceil = lceil 16.00006097 rceil = 17 text blocks  - **Data distribution across blocks:**     - The first 16 blocks will be full, each storing 65532 bytes of data.     - Total data stored in the full blocks:     16 × 65532 = 1048512 text bytes     - Remaining data to be stored in the last block:     1048576 - 1048512 = 64 text bytes     - Last block usage: 64 bytes (data) + 4 bytes (NULL pointer) = 68 bytes.  - **Internal fragmentation calculation:** Internal fragmentation is the unused space in the last allocated block:    textWasted space = textBlock size - ( textData in last block + textPointer in last block) = 2^16 - (64 + 4) = 65536 - 68 = 65468 text bytes Final Answer The amount of space wasted due to internal fragmentation is 65468 bytes.  ```c define K (1 << 10) define M (1 << 20) define BLOCK_SIZE (1 << 16) define POINTER_SIZE 4 define FILE_SIZE (1 << 20) \\int main() {     // Calculate usable data space per block int usable_data_space_per_block = BLOCK_SIZE - POINTER_SIZE;      // Calculate number of blocks required int num_blocks = (FILE_SIZE + usable_data_space_per_block - 1) / usable_data_space_per_block;      // Calculate total data stored in full blocks int total_data_in_full_blocks = num_blocks * usable_data_space_per_block;      // Calculate remaining data to be stored in the last block int remaining_data_in_last_block = FILE_SIZE - total_data_in_full_blocks;      // Calculate wasted space in the last block int wasted_space = BLOCK_SIZE - (remaining_data_in_last_block + POINTER_SIZE); return 0; } ``` Quick Tip To quickly solve this problem, focus on calculating the number of blocks required and the usable data space per block. Then, find the remaining data to be stored in the last block and subtract it from the block size to get the internal fragmentation."
