    explanation: "Derivation Let's evaluate the properties of **Recursive Languages** (also known as Decidable Languages).  - **A proper superset of context-free languages:** The Chomsky Hierarchy dictates that Context-Free Languages (CFLs) ⊂ Context-Sensitive Languages (CSLs) ⊂ Recursive Languages ⊂ Recursively Enumerable Languages (RE). Therefore, Recursive languages are indeed a proper superset of CFLs. (Option A is True).  - **Always recognizable by pushdown automata:** Pushdown Automata (PDAs) strictly recognize Context-Free Languages. They lack the full tape memory required to recognize all Recursive languages. (Option B is False).  - **Also called type 0 languages:** Type 0 languages in the Chomsky Hierarchy refer exclusively to Recursively Enumerable (RE) languages, not Recursive languages. (Option C is False).  - **Recognizable by Turing machines:** By definition, a language is Recursive if there exists a Turing Machine that always halts (accepts or rejects) on every input. Since a decider is a specific type of recognizer, they are absolutely recognizable by Turing Machines. (Option D is True). Therefore, Options A and D are correct. Common Trap Aspirants often confuse Recursive languages with Type 0 languages. Remember that Type 0 strictly maps to the outermost boundary of computation (Recursively Enumerable). Recursive languages sit just inside that boundary, ensuring safety (halting). AIR-1 Insight In terminology questions, strictly map the Chomsky numbering to the hardware limits: - Type 3 to Regular (DFA) - Type 2 to CFL (PDA) - Type 1 to CSL (LBA) - Type 0 to RE (TM) Recursive languages do not possess a dedicated \"Chomsky Number\", they exist in the conceptual void between Type 1 and Type 0. Quick Tip Eliminate options based on the Chomsky Hierarchy and Turing Machine recognition capabilities"
