    id: "PYQ3064", year: 2003, subject: "Theory of Computation", topic: "Undecidability",
    type: "MCQ", marks: 1,
    question: "Consider two languages \\( L_1 \\) and \\( L_2 \\) each on the alphabet \\(\\Sigma\\). Let \\( f: \\Sigma \\rightarrow \\Sigma \\) be a polynomial time computable bijection such that \\((\\forall x)[x \\in L_1 \\; \\text{iff} \\; f(x) \\in L_2]\\). Further, let \\( f^{-1} \\) be also polynomial time computable. Which of the following CANNOT be true?",
    image: null,
    options: ["L1 ∈ P and L2 is finite", "L1 ∈ NP and L2 ∈ P", "L1 is undecidable and L2 is decidable", "L1 is recursively enumerable and L2 is recursive"],
    answer: "L1 is undecidable and L2 is decidable",
    explanation: "Derivation We are given that f: Sigma to Sigma is a polynomial-time computable bijection, and f^-1 is also polynomial-time computable. The condition x ∈ L_1 iff f(x) ∈ L_2 establishes a **polynomial-time mapping reduction** between L_1 and L_2 in both directions (L_1 le_p L_2 and L_2 le_p L_1).  This means L_1 and L_2 are polynomially isomorphic and must share identical computational complexity boundaries.  Let's evaluate which option CANNOT be true:  - **Option 0:** L_1 ∈ P and L_2 is finite.   If L_2 is finite, it is decidable in O(1) time, so L_2 ∈ P. Because they are isomorphic, L_1 must also be in P. This matches the premise perfectly. (Can be true).  - **Option 1:** L_1 ∈ NP and L_2 ∈ P.   If L_2 ∈ P, then due to the polynomial reduction, L_1 must also be exactly in P. Since P ⊆ NP, a language that is in P is technically also in NP. Therefore, saying L_1 is in NP while L_2 is in P is not a mathematical contradiction. (Can be true).  - **Option 2:** L_1 is undecidable and L_2 is decidable.   If L_2 is decidable, we can build a decider for L_1: compute f(x) in poly-time, then run the decider for L_2. Thus, L_1 MUST be decidable. It is fundamentally impossible for them to cross the decidability boundary due to the reduction. (CANNOT be true).  - **Option 3:** L_1 is recursively enumerable and L_2 is recursive.   If L_2 is recursive (decidable), then L_1 must be recursive. Because every recursive language is also recursively enumerable, stating L_1 is RE while L_2 is recursive is not a contradiction. (Can be true).  Therefore, Option 2 represents an absolute mathematical impossibility.  Common Trap Aspirants select Option 1 because they assume P and NP are mutually exclusive labels, forgetting that P is a strict subset of NP. If a language is in P, claiming it is in NP is technically valid logic.  AIR-1 Insight Reductions lock two languages together across strict hardness boundaries. A bidirectional reduction (L_1 equiv L_2) means they can never sit on opposite sides of the absolute \"wall\" between Decidability and Undecidability.  Quick Tip For reduction problems, remember that if A reduces to B, then B is at least as 'hard' as A. This helps eliminate options."
