    question: "The set of all recursively enumerable languages is",
    image: null,
    options: ["closed under complementation", "closed under intersection.", "a subset of the set of all recursive languages.", "an uncountable set"],
    answer: "closed under intersection.",
    explanation: "Derivation Let's evaluate the mathematical properties of the set of all Recursively Enumerable (RE) languages.  - **Closure under Complementation:** If an RE language is closed under complement, its complement must also be RE. If both a language and its complement are RE, the language is inherently Recursive (Decidable). Because there exist RE languages that are not recursive (e.g., the Halting Problem), the class of RE languages is strictly **NOT closed under complementation**. (Option 0 is False).  - **Closure under Intersection:** If you have two RE languages, L_1 and L_2, you can build a Turing Machine that runs their respective recognizers in parallel (or sequentially). If a string is in the intersection, both recognizers will eventually halt and accept. Thus, the intersection is also RE. RE languages are **closed under intersection**. (Option 1 is True).  - **Subset of Recursive languages:** This is backwards. The set of Recursive languages is a strict subset of the set of RE languages. (Option 2 is False).  - **Uncountable set:** Turing Machines are defined by finite binary strings (encodings), making the set of all Turing Machines countably infinite (aleph_0). Consequently, the set of all RE languages is **countable**. (Option 3 is False).  Thus, only Option 1 is correct.  Common Trap Aspirants frequently swap the subset relationship between Recursive and Recursively Enumerable languages. Remember: Enumerable (Recognizable) is the larger, weaker class. Recursive (Decidable) is the smaller, stronger class.  AIR-1 Insight For closure properties of Turing Machines, use the \"Parallel Simulation\" thought experiment. You can always run two TMs simultaneously to find unions or intersections. But you can NEVER simulate a TM to find a complement, because if the original TM loops infinitely on rejection, you will never know when to halt and accept for the complement. Quick Tip RE languages are not closed under complement; use this to eliminate option A quickly."
